  "strategy": "1. **Approach and rationale**:\n   We hypothesize that the optimal solution for this problem may exhibit certain symmetries (rotationally symmetric to a lower degree) and structure. We will represent h using a two-scale analysis and synthesis approach:\n\n   - Basic idea: Use a sparse representation in the high-frequency bands.  \n   - Symmetry: Enforce reflection symmetry around x=1.  \n   - Objective: Minimize C\u2085 while maintaining.\n\n   Previous methods relied on full grid optimization, which suffers from high dimensionality. Our method reduces dimensions by exploiting structure, making local optimization more effective.\n\n2. **Mathematical specification**:\n   The function h(x) is represented using a pyramid multiresolution expansion:\n\n   \\[\n   h(x) = c_\u03d5( k_x) + \\sum_{j \u2208 J} d_j \u03c8_j (x) + \\sum_{k \u2208 R} r_k(x)\n   \\]\n\n   Where:\n   - \\( c_\u03d5(k_x) \\) is a coarse base function\n   - J is a sparse set of wavelet coefficients\n   - R is a residual correction\n\n   **Constraints and objective**: Same as original, with the reduction via symmetrization.\n\n3. **Implementation procedure**:\n   Phase 1: Pyramid fast expansion\n   \n   - Input n_points: integer, starting at 1000\n   - Discretize grid x_i = i/n_points * 2 - 1 (y-values [-1,1] for simplicity)\n   - Represent h(x) via 2-symmetric pyramid (ordered pyramid at level 2)\n   - Total variables = 20 (1 base + 19 coef)\n\n   Phase 2: Local optimization\n   \n   - Use gradient descent with finite differences\n   - Compute objective and its derivatives via automatic differentiation through the expansion\n   - Include warm-up on small grid to train AD\n\n   Phase 3: Multi-resolution scaling\n   \n   - Increment resolution by factor of 2 (until n_points reaches 4000)\n   - Re-initialize via projection from coarser scale\n\n   Phase 4: Final polishing\n   \n   - Run detailed optimization on maximum resolution allowed\n   - Compute true C\u2085 value to validate improvement\n\n4. **Search choices**:\n   - Grid density: \n       1000 \u2192 2000 \u2192 4000\n   - Optim time: 100s per resolution\n   - Variables: 20 base \u2192 400 final\n   - Symmetry: Forced only temporarily (removed if needed)\n\n5. **Time estimate**: \n   - Preprocessing: 20s\n   - 3 levels: ~900s\n   - Margin: 200s for final catch \n   - Total: ~1120s\n\n6. **Validation**: \n   - Check h in [0,1] after all changes\n   - Re-use phys_h for initial condition\n   - Compare to prior 0.380962\n   - Trigger if significantly worse",
